 "observations": "In this run we observed one single-file program (sith.py) grown from 1,603 to 7,288 lines across six sessions, each checked by hand rather than with a test suite. Sessions 2-4 read the whole file before editing (ck2 steps 2.2-2.4 sed ranges; ck3 3.3-3.7; ck4 4.2-4.6), while ck5 and ck6 started from a grep outline and read selected ranges (5.2-5.11, 6.3-6.15). Ck2 was a large rewrite: the engine section was redrafted in /tmp/ref/partB.py and spliced back in, keeping the original cursor helpers (2.12-2.14). Later sessions mostly added new functions (ck4 4.14, ck5 5.12, ck6 6.25). Verification relied on /tmp scratch projects in every session, a jedi reference venv in ck2 (2.6-2.10), and explicit old-vs-new regression diffs in ck2 (2.23), ck3 (3.20-3.24) and ck4 (4.23-4.37, 4,797 positions, 0 diffs). Ck5 extracted find_refs from references for reuse by rename (5.8). Of the 20 first-failures, most trace to spec gaps (None vs NoneType naming chosen to match jedi at 2.8; goto stub tests contradicting the ck3 import-statement default; env probing protocol at ck6). Three contradict stated spec text: circular-import visibility (ck3; tested by hand at 3.25 without catching it), namespace attribute type (ck6), and smart_sys_path (ck6). No regressions occurred."
